{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Surds: find the largest square number factor and simplify",
      "To rationalise: multiply numerator and denominator by the surd (or conjugate)",
      "Bounds: half a unit above and below the rounded value",
      "Max value of a/b: use upper a, lower b"
    ],
    "title": "Indices, Surds & Bounds",
    "content": "<p><strong>Surds</strong> are irrational square roots left in exact form, e.g. \\(\\sqrt{2}\\), \\(\\sqrt{5}\\). They cannot be written as exact decimals.</p><p><strong>Simplifying surds:</strong> Find the largest square factor: \\(\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\\). To add/subtract surds, they must have the same root: \\(3\\sqrt{2} + 5\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\\).</p><p><strong>Rationalising the denominator:</strong> Multiply top and bottom by the surd: \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). For \\(\\frac{1}{a + \\sqrt{b}}\\), multiply by the conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\).</p><p><strong>Error bounds:</strong> A measurement of \\(x\\) rounded to some degree of accuracy has bounds: \\(\\text{lower} \\leq x < \\text{upper}\\). For \\(5.3\\) to 1 d.p.: \\(5.25 \\leq x < 5.35\\). To maximise \\(\\frac{a}{b}\\), use upper \\(a\\) and lower \\(b\\).</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Simplify</strong> \\(\\sqrt{72}\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Largest square factor: \\(72 = 36 \\times 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(\\sqrt{36 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{36} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 6\\sqrt{2}\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(6\\sqrt{2}\\)</p>"
  },
